Package: logsh
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: sudosrv
Description: recording login shell for sudo I/O log servers
 logsh is a multi-call login shell that records a user's terminal session to a
 sudosrv log server, in the same sudoreplay-compatible format sudo produces.
 /usr/sbin/lbash and /usr/sbin/lzsh are symlinks to it; the name it is invoked
 under selects the real shell to run.
 .
 It belongs on every managed host, whereas the sudosrv daemon usually does not,
 which is why it is packaged separately. Installing it switches no account: see
 /usr/share/doc/logsh/logsh-deployment.md for the rollout and the mandatory
 break-glass drill before deploying anywhere you cannot walk up to.

Package: sudosrv
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: sudo (>= 1.9.0)
Description: sudo I/O log server
 A Go-based sudo I/O log server that implements the sudo_logsrv.proto
 protocol. It serves as an alternative to sudo's native sudo_logsrvd,
 capable of receiving and processing I/O logs from sudo clients
 (version 1.9.0+).
 .
 The server supports dual-mode operation: local storage mode (saves logs
 locally) or relay mode (forwards to upstream server). It includes full
 sudoers compatibility for log path customization, TLS support for secure
 log transmission, and store-and-forward caching when upstream servers
 are unavailable.